# .linkaudit-exclude — paths /link-audit-fix should not scan in this repo.
#
# One glob per line; `dir/**` excludes everything under dir. Blank lines and
# `#` comments ignored. Skipped files are always COUNTED AND REPORTED by the
# tool, so a scoped scan can never be mistaken for full coverage.
#
# Created 04-08-2026 (Opus 5, claude-opus-5[1m]) after this repo could not be
# link-audited at all: the scan hit a 900 s timeout and produced nothing.

# Imported OCR'd book text — 38 MB of the repo's 52 MB of tracked markdown, in
# 46 files. These are third-party publications (Routledge handbooks, Peshkovsky,
# Delbrück, …) mirrored here for reading, NOT project documentation:
#
#   * their `[...](...)` sequences are OCR artefacts and running text, not our
#     cross-repo references — they produced ~29,000 of ~31,000 "broken links",
#     none of which anyone would ever fix;
#   * four handbooks alone carry 5.4-7.1k self-anchors apiece.
#
# Excluding them makes the repo's audit both fast AND meaningful. If a genuine
# project doc ever lands under literature/, re-include it below rather than
# deleting the rule — the point is to drop imported text, not to hide a tree.
literature/**

# …but literature/ is NOT purely imported text, and a blanket exclude was wrong:
# it also dropped a cross-repo relevance map and a dated project README. Last
# matching pattern wins, so these come back into scope.
!literature/**/README.md
!literature/md/INDEX.md
